7592186045417] [17592186050843 #edb/ref 1031 "Simplifying Rational Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050843 #edb/ref 1033 68 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050843 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6ae775b6-8adc-58c2-abd8-8374a1314249" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186051017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1031 "Factoring Linear Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1033 87 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7f29909c-8f45-5a17-820a-13a0a327cd5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179467 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13903" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179467 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $2 = wz - 2w +1$ for the variable $w.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179467 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae7ee9c8-3162-564e-a3f2-f235b60527f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179467 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to isolate all terms with $w$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we subtract $1$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =wz-2w+1 \\\\ 2-1 & =wz-2w+1-1 \\\\ 1 & =wz-2w\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor out $w$ in the expression on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =wz-2w \\\\ 1 & =w(z-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $(z-2)$ to isolate $w\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =w(z-2) \\\\ \\frac{1}{z-2} & =\\frac{w(z-2)}{z-2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{z-2} & =\\frac{w\\cancel{(z-2)}}{\\cancel{z-2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{z-2} & =w \\\\ w & =\\frac{1}{z-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $z-2\\neq 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179469 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13905" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179469 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $4ky=5y + 1$ for the variable $y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179469 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f0aa09d-c765-5ab5-ac89-3fb4ce168d90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179469 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to isolate all terms with $y$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we subtract $5y$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4ky & =5y+1 \\\\ 4ky-5y & =5y+1-5y \\\\ 4ky-5y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $y$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $y$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nNext, we factor out $y$ in the expression on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4ky-5y & =1 \\\\ y(4k-5) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(4k -5)$ to isolate $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4k-5) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{y(4k-5)}{(4k-5)} & =\\frac{1}{(4k-5)} \\\\ \\frac{y\\cancel{(4k-5)}}{\\cancel{(4k-5)}} & =\\frac{1}{4k-5} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4k-5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $4k-5\\neq 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179471 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13907" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179471 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{xy}{3} = \\dfrac{y - 1}{2}$ for the variable $y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179471 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f9e1e805-32a3-5d25-8b09-36707350ab61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179471 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to isolate all terms with $y$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we first cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{xy}{3} & =\\frac{y-1}{2} \\\\ 2\\cdot xy & =3\\cdot (y-1) \\\\ 2xy & =3y-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $3y$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy & =3y-3 \\\\ 2xy-3y & =3y-3-3y \\\\ 2xy-3y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $y$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $y$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nThen, we factor out $y$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy-3y & =-3 \\\\ y(2x-3) & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(2x - 3)$ to isolate $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2x-3) & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{y(2x-3)}{2x-3} & =\\frac{-3}{2x-3} \\\\ \\frac{y\\cancel{(2x-3)}}{\\cancel{2x-3}} & =\\frac{-3}{2x-3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $2x-3\\neq 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179639 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179639 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179639 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179639 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179639 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179639 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179639 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179639 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving Linear Equations by Factoring" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179639 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179639 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "eade8768-b97c-5e90-ad02-255b9e8f8dae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179640 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179640 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179640 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179640 #edb/ref 1038 