tract $3q$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7pq & =3q+7 \\\\ 7pq-3q & =3q+7-3q \\\\ 7pq-3q & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $q$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $q$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nNext, we factor out $q$ in the expression on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7pq-3q & =7 \\\\ (7p-3)q & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(7p-3)$ to isolate $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(7p-3)q & =7 \\\\ \\frac{(7p-3)q}{7p-3} & =\\frac{7}{7p-3} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(7p-3)}q}{\\cancel{7p-3}} & =\\frac{7}{7p-3} \\\\ q & =\\frac{7}{7p-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $7p-3\\ne 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488213 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488213 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133899" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488213 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488213 #edb/ref 1071 "If $\\frac{2km}{3}=\\frac{k+m}{5},$ then $k=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488213 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5eedbb0b-2f52-5239-8343-cadc5e74eba1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488213 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to isolate all terms with $k$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we first cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2km}{3} & =\\frac{k+m}{5} \\\\ 5\\cdot 2km & =3\\cdot (k+m) \\\\ 10km & =3k+3m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $3k$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10km & =3k+3m \\\\ 10km-3k & =3k+3m-3k \\\\ 10km-3k & =3m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $k$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $k$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nThen, we factor out $k$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10km-3k & =3m \\\\ k(10m-3) & =3m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(10m-3)$ to isolate $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(10m-3) & =3m \\\\ \\frac{k\\cancel{(10m-3)}}{\\cancel{10m-3}} & =\\frac{3m}{10m-3} \\\\ k & =\\frac{3m}{10m-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $10m-3\\ne 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488221 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488221 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133790" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488221 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488221 #edb/ref 1071 "If $10=3rs+2s-2,$ then $s=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488221 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b9f4cd9-9a85-5f2d-9eac-f617eac7ba39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488221 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to isolate all terms with $s$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we add $2$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =3rs+2s-2 \\\\ 10+2 & =3rs+2s-2+2 \\\\ 12 & =3rs+2s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor out $s$ in the expression on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =3rs+2s \\\\ 12 & =s(3r+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $(3r+2)$ to isolate $s:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =s(3r+2) \\\\ \\frac{12}{3r+2} & =\\frac{s(3r+2)}{3r+2} \\\\ \\frac{12}{3r+2} & =\\frac{s\\cancel{(3r+2)}}{\\cancel{3r+2}} \\\\ \\frac{12}{3r+2} & =s \\\\ s & =\\frac{12}{3r+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $3r+2\\ne 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488231 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488231 #edb/ref 1069 "q-215810" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488231 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488231 #edb/ref 1071 "If $\\frac{7mn+2n}{4}=\\frac{3m}{7},$ then $m=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488231 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "77fe19c1-2d2e-5ba1-9d49-d82b50f369df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488231 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to isolate all terms with $m$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we first cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7mn+2n}{4} & =\\frac{3m}{7} \\\\ 7\\cdot (7mn+2n) & =4\\cdot 3m \\\\ 49mn+14n & =12m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $49mn$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}49mn+14n & =12m \\\\ 49mn+14n-49mn & =12m-49mn \\\\ 14n & =12m-49mn\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $m$ are on the right-hand side, and all terms independent of $m$ are on the left-hand side.\n\nThen, we factor out $m$ from the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14n & =12m-49mn \\\\ 14n & =(12-49n)m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(12-49n)$ to isolate $m:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14n & =(12-49n)m \\\\ \\frac{14n}{12-49n} & =\\frac{\\cancel{(12-49n)}m}{\\cancel{12-49n}} \\\\ \\frac{14n}{12-49n} & =m \\\\ m & =\\frac{14n}{12-49n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $12-49n\\ne 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488249 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488249 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133775" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488249 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488249 #edb/ref 1071 "If $5tx-3x=2,$ then $x=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488249 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a7db436d-ecf4-5342-922a-617ed93580d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488249 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor out $x$ in the expression on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5tx-3x & =2 \\\\ x(5t-3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $(5t-3)$ to isolate $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(5t-3) & =2 \\\\ \\frac{x(5t-3)}{5t-3} & =\\frac{2}{5t-3} \\\\ \\frac{x\\cancel{(5t-3)}}{\\cancel{5t-3}} & =\\frac{2}{5t-3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{5t-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $5t-3\\ne 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488267 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488267 #edb/ref 1069 "q-45310" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488267 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488267 #edb/ref 1071 "If $4c+5=3yc-2,$ then $c=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488267 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d1bde4eb-16c8-5f7c-8125-caf4a9cb1949" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488267 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to isolate all terms with $c$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we start by subtracting $5$ from both sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4c+5 & =3yc-2 \\\\ 4c+5-5 & =3yc-2-5 \\\\ 4c & =3yc-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $3yc$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4c & =3yc-7 \\\\ 4c-3yc & =3yc-7-3yc \\\\ 4c-3yc & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $c$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $c$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nNext, we factor out $c$ in the expression on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4c-3yc & =-7 \\\\ c(4-3y) & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(4-3y)$ to isolate $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c(4-3y) & =-7 \\\\ \\frac{c(4-3y)}{4-3y} & =\\frac{-7}{4-3y} \\\\ \\frac{c\\cancel{(4-3y)}}{\\cancel{4-3y}} & =-\\frac{7}{4-3y} \\\\ c & =\\frac{7}{3y-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $3y-4\\ne 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
